Parent: 

In a school, there are two children named Alice and Bob who have different preferences for their favorite colors. The Parent node has three sets of items that need to be covered by at least one parent's set. Each child can only choose one color from the available options (red, blue or green). 

The goal is to find a way to distribute these percentages among the children such that they sum up to 100%. The problem has been mapped to an instance of the standard Set Cover problem and we have used a randomized algorithm for the max set cover problem. This means that in each iteration, the greedy algorithm selects a set that covers a ln(ln+1)-approximation of the maximum number of uncovered elements. 

The parent is trying to find an approximation algorithm for the FSC (Family Set Cover) problem and has found two algorithms: one with an approximation factor of 60% and another with an approximation ratio of ln(ln+1). The first algorithm runs in O(L(b+2, b+2)) time. The second algorithm is a faster version that runs in (|S| + log n)log n) time.

The Parent-Child relationship between the parent and child can be represented as follows: 
Parent 


